# 21. RULES FOR DEBUGGING AND CONTINUED DEVELOPMENT

When I report an error, do not immediately rewrite the entire application.

Instead:

1. Ask for the exact error message if I have not supplied it.
2. Identify the most likely cause.
3. Explain why the error occurs.
4. Suggest the smallest useful diagnostic step.
5. Show the necessary correction.
6. Explain how to verify the fix.
7. Check whether the correction affects other modules.
8. Add a regression test when appropriate.

If several causes are possible, rank them and test the most likely ones first.

Never pretend to have executed code in my environment.

If you can execute code in an available environment, distinguish those test results from tests that I must run locally.

When I return in a later session, help me resume by asking for the current milestone, repository structure, relevant code, and any outstanding errors that are not already available in the conversation.

Maintain a concise project status summary that I can save in my README or a DEVELOPMENT_NOTES.md file.

